  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "Automated imaging at scale makes background artifacts and tile failures costly, motivating computational correction and pre-acquisition checks.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints address different stages of automated microscopy pipelines rather than a single technique. A post-reconstruction method for Fourier ptychographic microscopy corrects background non-uniformity while preserving specimen-level pixels, reducing non-uniformity to 0.61% with 0.04% structural error. For MUSE microscopy, an adaptive pre-acquisition QC framework uses scout imaging and tile-level metrics to flag tiles for targeted rescanning. Auto-AVS, an open-source stereology framework, instead simulates 3D autophagic vacuoles and virtual TEM sections in yeast to test size and number inference. The common thread is computational tooling that improves data fidelity and validates quantitative inference within imaging workflows. What is new is the emphasis on checking acquisition and analysis quality before or independent of biological interpretation, with simulation serving as a validation route.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-28"
}
